<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ganja, Azerbaijan</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Elizavetpol, Kirovabad</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ganja is the second-largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>, in the west of the country. A major centre of the medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>, it was the home of the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizami-ganjavi">Nizami Ganjavi</a> in the 12th century. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Shah</a>'s death it became the seat of an independent khanate, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> stormed in 1804; renamed Elizavetpol, the city took back the name Ganja in 1918 and, after a Soviet period as Kirovabad, again in 1989.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ganja-azerbaijan.jpg" alt="Ganja, Azerbaijan" width="320" height="245" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NordNordWest, edited by Toghrul R · CC BY-SA 3.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ganja_City_in_Azerbaijan.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Ganja lies 400 to 450 metres above sea level on the Ganja-Dashkasan plain in the west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>, 375 km from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baku">Baku</a>. It stands at the north-eastern foothills of the Lesser Caucasus on the Ganjachay river. The name comes from the Persian word ganj, meaning treasure or treasury; Arabic sources call the city Janza and Armenian sources Gandzak. The tale that its founder found treasure nearby is considered a folk etymology.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The anonymous Arabic history Tarikh Bab al-abwab says Ganja was founded in 859–60 by Muhammad ibn Khalid, the Arab governor of the region under the caliph Al-Mutawakkil, after he unearthed a treasure there. The medieval Armenian historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/movses-kaghankatvatsi">Movses Kaghankatvatsi</a> supports a 9th-century Arab foundation, while the Persian name suggests an older pre-Islamic town. From the 9th to the 12th century the area was known as Arran, and its urban population mainly spoke Persian. The city was damaged by an earthquake in September 1139, when King Demetrius I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> took it and carried off its gates, and again by the Mongol invasion of 1231. It revived after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavids</a> came to power in 1501 and was occupied briefly by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> in 1578–1606 and 1723–1735.</p><p>After the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Shah</a> in 1747 Ganja became the centre of the Ganja Khanate, whose khans were subordinate to the rule of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. Russia, after annexing eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> in 1801, met strong opposition at Ganja. In 1804 Russian forces under General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pavel-tsitsianov">Pavel Tsitsianov</a> invaded and sacked the city, sparking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-persian-war-1804-1813">Russo-Persian War</a> of 1804–1813. Some western sources claim that up to 3,000 inhabitants were massacred. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-gulistan">Treaty of Gulistan</a> forced Iran to cede the khanate, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-turkmenchay">Treaty of Turkmenchay</a> of 1828 made its inclusion in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> permanent. It was renamed Yelizavetpol after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-i-of-russia">Alexander I</a>'s wife, Elizabeth, and became capital of the Elizavetpol Governorate in 1868, though local Azerbaijanis continued to call it Ganja.</p><p>In 1918 Ganja was the temporary capital of the Azerbaijan Democratic Republic. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> occupied Azerbaijan in April 1920, and in May an anti-Soviet rebellion in Ganja was put down, heavily damaging the city. In 1935 it was renamed Kirovabad after Sergei Kirov, and it regained its original name in 1989. A pogrom in November 1988 forced the local Armenian population to leave.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Ganja is a major city of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>, divided into the Kapaz and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizami-ganjavi">Nizami</a> districts, which were established in 1980. The poet Nizami Ganjavi was born there, and its old landmarks include the Nizami Mausoleum, the Shah Abbas Caravanserai, the Imamzadeh complex and the Chokak Hamam. Most of its people are Muslim, mainly Shia, and most of its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and Germans have emigrated. The city is known for Ganja silk, and metallurgical and food industries operate there.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ganja, Azerbaijan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The First Nagorno-Karabakh War with the region's Armenian majority, backed by Armenia, lasted until 1994; both sides committed atrocities and pogroms, among them the Khojaly massacre, the Baku pogrom, the Maraga massacre and the <b>Kirovabad</b> pogrom.</blockquote><a href="/empire/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After Nader Shah's death in 1747, Shahverdi Khan of the Ziyadoghlu branch of the Qajar tribe, whose family had long governed <b>Ganja</b> and Karabakh for the Safavids, made Ganja an independent khanate.</blockquote><a href="/state/ganja-khanate">Ganja Khanate</a></li></ul></section>
